<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maldives</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Maldives, Maldive Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maldives, officially the Republic of Maldives, is an archipelagic country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, made up of 26 atolls. It has been inhabited for over 2,500 years. Its last Buddhist king, Dhovemi, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1153 (or 1193) and began a series of six Islamic dynasties. The Portuguese set up a garrison in 1558, and a local revolt later ended their presence. The islands became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1887, gained independence from the United Kingdom in 1965, and became a republic in 1968 after a referendum.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Maldives is a chain of 26 atolls stretching across the equator from Ihavandhippolhu Atoll in the north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addu-atoll">Addu Atoll</a> in the south, about 750 kilometres from the Asian mainland and south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Its land area is only 298 square kilometres, spread over roughly 90,000 square kilometres of sea, which makes it one of the most spatially dispersed sovereign states and the smallest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The average ground-level elevation is around 1.5 metres and the highest natural point is only 2.4 metres, so it is the world's lowest-lying country. Malé, traditionally called the King's Island, is the capital and most populated city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kingdoms already existed in the 6th–5th century BCE, and several artefacts show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> was present before the Islamic period. The Buddhist period lasted 1,400 years, and the Maldivian language, early scripts and ruling institutions originated then. Documented contact with the outside world began around 947 CE, when Arab travellers began visiting. The last Buddhist king, Dhovemi, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1153 (or 1193) and took the title Sultan Muhammad al-Adil. He began six Islamic dynasties that lasted until 1932.</p><p>In 1558 the Portuguese set up a small garrison under a Viador, or overseer of a trading post, administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>. Their attempts to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under threat of death provoked a revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-thakurufaanu">Muhammad Thakurufaanu</a> al-A'uẓam, his two brothers and Dhuvaafaru Dhandahele, who drove the Portuguese out fifteen years later. The islands became a British protectorate in 1887. An agreement of 26 July 1965 ended British authority over defence and external affairs, and the sultanate lasted three more years under Sir Muhammad Fareed Didi. A tsunami on 26 December 2004 caused damage estimated at more than US$400 million, about 62 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a>; 102 Maldivians and 6 foreigners reportedly died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Maldives is a presidential republic, established in 1968, with an elected People's Majlis. Islam is the official religion, and the constitution of 2008 states that the republic is based on the principles of Islam. The country is divided into twenty-one administrative divisions, each atoll governed by an elected Atoll Council. Tourism is now the largest economic sector, ahead of fishing, which was historically dominant. It is a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a> and the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maldives” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rajaraja I's naval campaigns captured Sri Lanka, the <b>Maldives</b> and the Malabar Coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamil language and culture spread beyond Tamilakam, to the <b>Maldives</b>, Sri Lanka and some coasts of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1919 to 1945 it carried the anthem of the Korean exile government, from 1945 to 1948 South Korea's national anthem was sung to it, and the <b>Maldives</b> used it for their anthem until 1972.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li></ul></section>
